      "writtenOn": "2021-06-28T07:48:27Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Per comment above: if the \"force USB function\" setting changed since it was read at line 1425, then this type might be incorrect. Can you add a comment explaining what can happen?\n\nI think one thing that can happen is that if someone starts TETHERING_USB and we pick NCM, but then the config changes to us RNDIS for tethering, then this code won\u0027t start any IpServers, but the NCM function will remain enabled. I don\u0027t know what happens after that if someone stops TETHERING_NCM, or stops TETHERING_USB, or if the SettingsObserver calls stopTetheringInternal for the functions. Will the system crash? ...? Can you look into it?\n\nIf nothing particularly bad happens, then maybe we can just add a comment here explaining what happens.\n\nThat said - per earlier comment it seems more correct to put this in the TetheringRequest. As soon as a request for USB or NCM tethering was processed, we could check the configuration and decide what function to enable (including enabling no functions and returning an error if NCM is requested but the config option to use NCM for TETHERING_USB is enabled). Then, when handleUsbAction is called, the code can look at the existing requests to see which one matches the function that was enabled. That will tell the code what tethering type was requested, without having to guess it from the configuration. For teardown, we could have the IpServer store the TetheringRequestParcel. That way, disableUsbIpServing could just get the function to disable by asking the IpServer for what function was in the original request.\n\nMaybe add a TODO for this?",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 1335,
        "startChar": 34,
        "endLine": 1335,
        "endChar": 53
      },
      "revId": "bfcb4cf6c56c0ca49c5bcec4c0f7002b6c24eaf1",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
